*** Working notes ***

Back in 2006 I started reworking SSM for portability (I had a new mac!),
more plugin backends, sub-patches, a better audio/GUI split, and a move away
from FLTK. Much of it worked in various branches, then disappeared along with
SVN access, darcs repos, and a hard drive.

Life happened. Bad decisions were made.

In 2024 I recovered scattered pieces from roughly four(?) hard drives and
started working on them sporadically. More recently, Grok and now Astra have
helped reconstruct the history and lost work.

The private repos contain years of UI ports and engine experiments in various
states of usefulness. I am not publishing all of that.

This public repo brings the best pieces back onto the abandoned upstream,
alongside patches from other people and new fixes.

AI has been useful for build fixes, stack "modernizing" and certain bugs,
sometimes better than my original work. Sometimes.

(side rant: modern stacks are SO BAD. Enshitification + Wirth's law in action)

Architectural changes still tend to involve burning through tokens,
then doing it myself anyway.

This past week cost $100 on Grok, $100 and three resets on Astra,
and still left me untangling a reconstructed git history
that bounced from 2026 to 2006 to 2026 to 2008 etc. Argh.

(Oct 8 2026: those dates are repaired. Commits that replayed old work now
carry the date they were replayed, with the original kept in an Original-Date
trailer, so the log reads forwards. Clones from before that need a fresh one.)


*** Currently included ***

- libspiralcore
- my 2006 autotools work with subsequent updates
- community patches not already incorporated
- 2007 + 2026 plugin splitting partial work
- a new audio callback output model
- more audio backends (coreaudio, jack, pipewire, portaudio, alsa, oss, dummy)
- more midi backends (coreaudio, alsa, jack(?), rtmidi, dummy)
- an atomic clock and a presentation clock
- more 2026 plugin splitting
- FLTK fixes
- load format fixes using a contract reader (incomplete, but covers all the
  old patches I have left)
- an unsupported device that preserves missing plugins/ladspa through a save,
  when ladspa is missing on load
- better support for ladspa loads where port numbers have changed
- a new bundled project format, supporting save points (each a new copy/assets)
- ???
- FreeBSD, OpenBSD, linux x86_64 / arm, glibc/musl, and macOS fixes

- Bundles swh ladspa plugins by default, including mac.

The install path is the spiral plugins folder.

Should take priority over system installed where applicable.

Reason: Hermes has been testing and building for release all morning,
working through the lab and build fixes, and ran into mac issues
without ports etc, no ladspa.

LADSPA is a frozen header anyway, and then the bundled example.
So because of missing gverb, and swh has the most coverage,
installing by default for now. 

Tested on Void Linux with musl and an M1 Mac running macOS 14.

Some things tested using CI without my asking by ChatGPT on Ubuntu and others.

(Burning through all my github actions at once....)

And a new lab of VMs, using AI to run tests on various platforms and stacks.

Still very much a work in progress.


