*** Building ***

Three steps, as it has always been: configure, make, make install. What has
changed is how little is required and how much is optional.

Needed: a C++ compiler and FLTK (1.3 or 1.4; if you build FLTK from source
use --enable-shared). That is the whole list. Everything else is detected,
and configure prints what it found and what it skipped when it finishes.

Found if present, skipped with a reason if not:

  audio out     JACK, ALSA, OSS, PortAudio, PipeWire, ESD, and on macOS a
                native CoreAudio client built from the system frameworks
  midi          ALSA sequencer, JACK MIDI, RtMidi, CoreMIDI
  files         libsndfile, for everything that loads or writes audio:
                PoshSampler, Streamer and SpiralLoop read any format it does
  LADSPA        the plugin host needs only ladspa.h, and a copy is in the
                tree, so it builds whether or not the system has one
  liblrdf       LADSPA plugin metadata, if you have it
  FFTW          single precision, for four of the bundled LADSPA plugins

Turn any of them off with --disable-NAME: --disable-jack, --disable-ladspa,
--disable-sndfile, --disable-lrdf, --disable-portaudio, --disable-pipewire,
--disable-alsa-output, --disable-oss-output, --disable-coreaudio,
--disable-rtmidi, --disable-alsa-midi, --disable-jack-midi, --disable-esd.

--disable-bundled-ladspa leaves out the vendored swh-plugins, for packagers
who would rather depend on their distribution's copy. With them in, they are
found ahead of any system copy of the same plugin, and the system LADSPA
directories stay on the search path behind them.

--with-plugindir=DIR puts the plugins somewhere other than
$libdir/SpiralPlugins/$VERSION. They install as dsp/, panels/, audio/, midi/
and ladspa/ under it, and the application looks for them there.

--enable-debug for a debugging build.

There is no flag for choosing the GUI toolkit. The macOS build uses Cocoa
through FLTK because that is what FLTK does there; the Qt and GTK hosts are
in the private tree and not in this one yet.

From a release tarball:

  ./configure
  make
  make install
  SpiralSynthModular

From a git checkout, run ./autogen.sh first; a tarball is already configured.
Out of tree builds work and are the easier way to keep several configurations
around:

  ./autogen.sh
  mkdir build && cd build
  ../configure && make

make check runs the test suite. The FLTK window tests need a display; under
ssh set DISPLAY, or they fail with "Can't open display".

On macOS, make bundle wraps the build as SpiralSynthModular.app with its
libraries and plugins inside, from MacPorts or any prefix given to configure
through CPPFLAGS and LDFLAGS.

Then try the designs in Examples: File / Examples lists them. Looking Backward
into the Future.ssmp is an SSM Project with three save points and the credits
and licence of the track it came from; About Patch on the canvas menu shows
them.

usage:
SpiralSynthModular options [patch.ssm | project.ssmp]

options list:
-h : help
-v : print version
--NoGUI : run without GUI (only useful when loading patch from command line)
--Realtime : spawn audio thread with FIFO scheduling (run as root)
--PluginPath <PATH> : look for plugins in the specified directory


*** Setting up your .spiralmodular file ***

This file contains all the setup variables for the program. Thes values can be
edited with the options window whilst running SSM.
The following variables in the are the ones to play with to get a good playback
on your machine. This is usually tradeoff between quality and responsiveness
(latency). You can build designs and songs at low quality (22050 samplerate etc)
and then record them at higher settings. The wav file will sound fine, even if
the realtime output doesn't.

BufferSize        = 512         - Size of sample buffer to be calculated each cycle.
FragmentSize      = 256         - Subbuffer size sent to the soundcard
FragmentCount     = 8           - Subbuffer count, -1 computes a suitable size,
                                  setting to 4 or 8 gets better latencies.
Samplerate        = 44100       - Sets the samplerate


